---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const emailFatigue = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Ad%20fatigue%20prevention%20%E2%80%94%20tell%20us%20about%20your%20ads";
const emailGeneral = "mailto:olga@okad.agency?subject=Ad%20fatigue%20inquiry";
const calHref = "https://calendly.com/okad_calls/first";
---

<Layout
  title="Ad Fatigue &mdash; The Complete Guide to Creative Decay & Prevention | OKAD"
  description="Ad fatigue is the decline in ad performance caused by audience overexposure. Meta's Andromeda system has compressed creative lifespan to roughly 10 days. This guide covers the signs, the timeline, platform-specific decay rates, and how to build a prevention pipeline."
>
  <Header />
  <main>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="pt-20 md:pt-24 pb-10 text-center bg-bg">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-[12px] font-medium text-accent bg-[rgba(99,102,241,.06)] border border-[rgba(99,102,241,.18)] rounded-full px-3.5 py-1.5 mb-6">
          Open reference &middot; September 2026
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(28px,5vw,48px)] leading-[1.1] tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink mb-4">
          Ad Fatigue: <span class="text-accent">The Complete Guide</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-[18px] text-ink-2 max-w-[600px] mx-auto leading-[1.6] mb-8">
          Ad fatigue is the gradual decline in ad performance caused by audience overexposure. Meta&rsquo;s Andromeda system has compressed creative lifespan from 6 weeks to roughly 10 days. This guide covers the signs, the timeline, and how to build a pipeline that outpaces decay.
        </p>
        <div class="flex gap-3 justify-center flex-wrap mb-8">
          <a href={emailFatigue} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-accent text-white hover:bg-accent-hover transition-all no-underline">Get a fatigue audit &rarr;</a>
          <a href={calHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-sans text-[14px] font-semibold bg-card text-ink border border-border hover:border-accent hover:text-accent transition-all no-underline">Book a call &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <div class="flex gap-5 flex-wrap justify-center font-mono text-[12px] text-muted">
          <span>Paid ads</span>
          <span>Creative decay</span>
          <span>~10-day lifespan</span>
          <span>Frequency &gt;2.5</span>
          <span>Continuous pipeline</span>
          <span>6&ndash;12 concepts/week</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- How it works grid -->
    <section class="py-8">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <h2 class="font-display text-[22px] font-bold tracking-[-0.02em] text-center mb-5">How Ad Fatigue Works</h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3.5">
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F4C9;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Performance Declines</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">CTR drops 30&ndash;50% by days 8&ndash;10. CPA rises. The algorithm deprioritizes stale creative in favor of fresher ads.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x23F3;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Compressed Lifespan</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">Meta&rsquo;s Andromeda system (2024+) saturates audiences faster. Creative lifespan dropped from 6 weeks to ~10 days on average.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-card border border-border rounded-[10px] p-4 text-center">
            <div class="text-[22px] mb-1.5">&#x1F504;</div>
            <h3 class="font-display text-[14px] font-semibold mb-1">Pipeline Is the Only Fix</h3>
            <p class="text-[12px] text-muted leading-[1.4]">You can&rsquo;t prevent fatigue entirely. You prevent the gap between winners with a continuous testing pipeline: 6&ndash;12 new concepts/week.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <section class="py-10 border-t border-b border-border">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-muted-2 mb-4">Contents</div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-1.5">
          {[
            { num: "01", text: "Overview", href: "#overview" },
            { num: "02", text: "What Is Ad Fatigue?", href: "#what" },
            { num: "03", text: "The Fatigue Timeline", href: "#timeline" },
            { num: "04", text: "Signs of Fatigue", href: "#signs" },
            { num: "05", text: "Fatigue vs Poor Creative", href: "#diagnostic" },
            { num: "06", text: "The Andromeda Effect", href: "#andromeda" },
            { num: "07", text: "Platform-Specific Decay Rates", href: "#platforms" },
            { num: "08", text: "Extending Creative Life", href: "#extending" },
            { num: "09", text: "The Prevention Pipeline", href: "#pipeline" },
            { num: "10", text: "Fatigue Monitoring System", href: "#monitoring" },
            { num: "11", text: "Glossary", href: "#glossary" },
            { num: "12", text: "FAQ", href: "#faq" },
          ].map((item) => (
            <a href={item.href} class="flex items-center gap-2.5 px-3 py-2 rounded-lg text-[14px] text-ink-2 font-medium hover:bg-bg-alt transition-colors no-underline">
              <span class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium text-accent min-w-[22px]">{item.num}</span>
              {item.text}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01 Overview -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="overview">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">01 &mdash; Overview</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Every winning ad has an expiration date</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Ad fatigue is the inevitable decline in performance from audience overexposure</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">On Meta in 2026, most creatives peak within 3&ndash;7 days and die by day 14&ndash;21</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The only structural solution is a continuous creative pipeline</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Ad fatigue is the single biggest reason ad accounts plateau. You find a winning creative, scale it, watch CPA drop &mdash; and then, within days or weeks, performance starts sliding. CTR drops. CPA rises. The algorithm quietly deprioritizes your ad. By the time most teams notice, the creative is already dead.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This isn&rsquo;t a bug in the system. It&rsquo;s a feature. Ad platforms are designed to show users fresh, relevant content. When the same user sees the same ad for the fifth time, they scroll past. The platform notices and shifts impressions elsewhere. Your ad enters a death spiral: fewer impressions &rarr; worse delivery &rarr; higher CPA &rarr; even fewer impressions.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Understanding fatigue is essential for anyone running paid ads. Not because you can prevent it &mdash; you can&rsquo;t &mdash; but because you can build a system that outpaces it. The brands that win on paid media are not the ones with one brilliant ad. They are the ones with a pipeline that produces new winners faster than old ones decay.</p>
        <ul class="list-none p-0 space-y-2">
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">What:</strong> gradual decline in ad performance from audience overexposure</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">When:</strong> CTR drops 30&ndash;50% by days 8&ndash;10 on Meta</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Trigger:</strong> frequency exceeds 2.5&ndash;3.0 in the target audience</li>
          <li class="flex items-baseline gap-2 py-1.5 text-ink-2"><strong class="text-ink min-w-[120px]">Solution:</strong> continuous creative pipeline: 6&ndash;12 new concepts/week</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02 What Is Ad Fatigue -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="what">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">02 &mdash; Definition</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">What is ad fatigue?</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Audience sees the same ad too many times and stops responding</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Platform algorithms detect declining engagement and reduce delivery</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Not the same as a bad creative &mdash; fatigue happens to winners, not losers</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Ad fatigue</strong> is the measurable decline in ad performance caused by repeated exposure to the same creative. It affects every ad that runs long enough, on every platform. The mechanism is straightforward: when a user has seen your ad three, four, five times, they develop &ldquo;banner blindness&rdquo; &mdash; they scroll past without registering it. The platform detects this declining engagement and reduces the ad&rsquo;s delivery priority.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The frequency threshold</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Fatigue correlates strongly with <strong>frequency</strong> &mdash; the average number of times each user in your audience has seen the ad. The critical threshold on Meta is a frequency of <strong>2.5&ndash;3.0</strong>. Once your ad crosses this threshold, you will see CTR begin to drop, CPA begin to rise, and delivery volume decrease. On TikTok, fatigue sets in faster &mdash; sometimes at a frequency of 1.5&ndash;2.0 &mdash; because the platform&rsquo;s content feed moves faster and users expect constant novelty.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Fatigue vs failure</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">This distinction is critical. A <em>bad</em> creative never performs well &mdash; its CPA is above target from day one. A <em>fatigued</em> creative performed well and then declined. The data signatures are completely different, and the response should be different too. A bad creative should be killed and analyzed. A fatigued creative should be iterated &mdash; its core concept is proven; it just needs a fresh execution. See <a href="#diagnostic" class="text-accent hover:underline">Section 05</a> for the diagnostic framework.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">What fatigue looks like in the data</h3>
        <ul class="pl-5 mb-4 space-y-2 text-ink-2">
          <li><strong>CTR drops 30&ndash;50%</strong> from peak by days 8&ndash;10</li>
          <li><strong>CPA increases 40&ndash;80%</strong> from its best-performing window</li>
          <li><strong>Frequency climbs past 2.5</strong> in the target audience</li>
          <li><strong>Impression volume decreases</strong> as the algorithm deprioritizes the ad</li>
          <li><strong>CPM stays flat or rises</strong> &mdash; you&rsquo;re paying more per impression for worse results</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03 The Fatigue Timeline -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="timeline">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">03 &mdash; Timeline</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">The fatigue timeline: launch to death</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Launch &rarr; Peak (days 3&ndash;7) &rarr; Plateau (days 7&ndash;14) &rarr; Decline &rarr; Death</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">On Meta in 2026, the full cycle takes roughly 10&ndash;21 days</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Higher spend compresses the timeline &mdash; $1K/day fatigues faster than $100/day</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Every winning ad follows a predictable lifecycle. Understanding each phase helps you make better decisions about when to iterate, when to extend, and when to replace.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Phase</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Timing (Meta)</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">What happens</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Action</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #1565c0">Launch</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Days 1&ndash;3</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Algorithm explores audiences. CPA volatile, data unreliable.</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Wait. Don&rsquo;t make decisions yet.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #2e7d32">Peak</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Days 3&ndash;7</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Best CPA window. Algorithm found the right audiences. CTR highest.</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Scale gradually (20&ndash;30%/day). Start producing iterations.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #f57f17">Plateau</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Days 7&ndash;14</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">CPA stable but no longer improving. Frequency approaching 2.0. First signs of decay.</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Launch fresh variations. Prepare replacements.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #e65100">Decline</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Days 14&ndash;21</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">CTR dropping steadily. CPA 30&ndash;60% above peak. Frequency past 2.5.</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Reduce spend. Move budget to new winners.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink font-semibold" style="color: #c62828">Death</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Day 21+</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">CPA 2&ndash;3&times; peak. Algorithm barely delivering. Impressions near zero.</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Kill. Archive learnings. Move on.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Budget accelerates fatigue</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">A creative running at $100/day might last 4&ndash;6 weeks. The same creative at $1,000/day will fatigue in 1&ndash;2 weeks. Higher spend means the algorithm exhausts your audience faster. This creates a paradox for scaling: the more you spend on a winner, the faster it dies. The only way out is to have new winners ready before the current one decays.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">Audience size matters</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Narrower audiences fatigue faster because there are fewer people to show the ad to. A lookalike audience of 1M people will fatigue faster than a broad audience of 20M. If you&rsquo;re running niche targeting, expect shorter creative lifespans and plan accordingly.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 04 Signs of Fatigue -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="signs">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">04 &mdash; Signs</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">How to spot fatigue before it kills performance</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Frequency climbing past 2.5 is the earliest reliable signal</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">CTR decline precedes CPA increase by 2&ndash;3 days</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Monitor daily &mdash; weekly reviews miss the decline window</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Fatigue doesn&rsquo;t arrive suddenly. It follows a predictable sequence of signals. If you monitor the right metrics daily, you can catch fatigue early enough to act &mdash; before CPA spikes wreck your weekly numbers.</p>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The signal sequence (in order of appearance)</h3>
        <ol class="list-none p-0 counter-reset-step space-y-0">
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Frequency rises past 2.0.</strong> The audience is seeing your ad more often. This is the earliest warning. You have 3&ndash;5 days before performance impact.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>CTR begins to drop.</strong> Users who have seen the ad before scroll past it. CTR typically drops 15&ndash;25% before CPA is affected.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Hook rate declines.</strong> Even the first 3 seconds stop holding attention. The audience recognizes the creative instantly and swipes away. See our <a href="/hook-rate" class="text-accent hover:underline">hook rate guide</a> for benchmarks.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>CPA increases.</strong> This is the metric most teams notice, but it&rsquo;s a lagging indicator. By the time CPA has risen 30%+, fatigue has been active for several days.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative border-b border-border text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>Impression volume drops.</strong> The algorithm has decided your ad is no longer competitive. Delivery decreases even if your bid and budget remain the same.</li>
          <li class="py-3.5 pl-[52px] relative text-ink-2 guide-step"><strong>CPM rises or stays flat while volume drops.</strong> You&rsquo;re paying the same (or more) per impression for increasingly worse results. The ad is effectively dead.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The 3-day rule</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">If CTR drops for 3 consecutive days and frequency is above 2.0, fatigue has started. Don&rsquo;t wait for CPA to confirm it. Begin rotating in fresh creative immediately while reducing spend on the fatiguing ad. Waiting for weekly reviews to catch this pattern costs 3&ndash;5 days of wasted spend.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05 Fatigue vs Poor Creative -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="diagnostic">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">05 &mdash; Diagnostic</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Fatigue vs poor creative: the diagnostic framework</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Fatigue shows a decline curve &mdash; performance was good, then dropped</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Poor creative shows flat underperformance from launch</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">The response is different: iterate fatigued concepts, kill bad ones</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">One of the most expensive mistakes in paid media is misdiagnosing fatigue as a bad creative (and vice versa). A fatigued creative&rsquo;s underlying concept is proven &mdash; it should be iterated, not abandoned. A poor creative should be killed and analyzed, not given more time. Here&rsquo;s how to tell the difference.</p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-border bg-card mb-6">
          <table class="w-full border-collapse text-[14px]">
            <thead class="bg-bg">
              <tr>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Signal</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Ad fatigue</th>
                <th class="font-display font-semibold text-[13px] px-4 py-3 text-left border-b border-border">Poor creative</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Performance curve</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Good &rarr; declining</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Bad from day 1</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CPA trend</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Below target initially, rising over time</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Above target from launch</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Frequency</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">High (2.5+)</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Low (1.0&ndash;1.5)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Hook rate trend</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Was good, now declining</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Low from start</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">CTR trend</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Declining over time</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Consistently low</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Time running</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">7+ days</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2">Any duration</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink">Correct response</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2 font-semibold" style="color: #2e7d32">Iterate the concept with fresh execution</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 border-b border-border text-ink-2 font-semibold" style="color: #c62828">Kill and analyze why</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink">Concept value</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Proven &mdash; the angle works</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-ink-2">Unproven &mdash; the angle may not resonate</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3 class="font-display font-semibold text-[20px] tracking-[-0.01em] mt-8 mb-3">The iteration opportunity</h3>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">When a creative fatigues, you have a proven concept. The messaging angle, the core hook idea, the value proposition &mdash; all validated by real purchase data. The execution (specific footage, specific creator, specific edit) is stale. The fix is a fresh execution of the same concept: new hook variant, different creator, updated visuals, same underlying message. This is the core logic behind <a href="/modular-creative" class="text-accent hover:underline">modular creative production</a> &mdash; building components that can be recombined into fresh variations.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

